# Fetch dashboard

Loading, error and retry states around a typed `Get`, plus a page-wide
`When a fetch fails` counter independent of how each caller handles its own
`Err`.

## Note

The error banner shows a fixed message rather than `e's message`: reading a
typed fetch's `Err` binding's own field crashes the WASM AOT backend for any
`Ok` type (`work/lgx-ease/gauntlet.md` finding G10) — this is the documented
pattern in `docs/frontend.md`'s Fetching section, not a guess.

## A Metric has:
    a label, which is Text.
    a value, which is Int.

## Page /

State:
    Let status be "idle".
    Let metrics be a new Seq of Metric.
    Let lastError be "".
    Let failedFetches be 0.

Actions:
    To load:
        Set status to "loading".
        Get "/api/metrics" as Seq of Metric, then loaded.

    To retry:
        Set status to "loading".
        Get "/api/metrics" as Seq of Metric, then loaded.

    To loaded (answer: Result of Seq of Metric and HttpError):
        Inspect answer:
            When Ok (list):
                Set metrics to list.
                Set status to "loaded".
            When Err (e):
                Set status to "failed".
                Set lastError to "Something went wrong. Try again.".

    When a fetch fails (url: Text) and (error: Text):
        Set failedFetches to failedFetches + 1.

View:
    <main class="dashboard">
      <h1>Dashboard</h1>
      <button id="load" on-click={load}>Load metrics</button>
      <p id="status">{status}</p>
      {if status is equal to "loading":}
        <p id="spinner">Loading…</p>
      {end}
      {if status is equal to "failed":}
        <p id="error">{lastError}</p>
        <button id="retry" on-click={retry}>Retry</button>
      {end}
      <ul>
        {for each m in metrics, keyed by m's label:}
          <li id="metric-{m's label}">{m's label}: {m's value}</li>
        {end}
      </ul>
      <p id="fail-count">{failedFetches} failed fetches</p>
    </main>

Style:
    .dashboard { max-width: 28rem; margin: 3rem auto; }
    #error { color: var(--accent); }

## Test: idle before anything is loaded

Expect that the text of "#status" is equal to "idle".
Expect that the count of "li" is equal to 0.

## Test: a successful load shows the metrics

Given that GET "/api/metrics" answers 200 with "[{{\"label\": \"users\", \"value\": 42}}, {{\"label\": \"errors\", \"value\": 0}}]".
Click "#load".
Expect that the text of "#status" is equal to "loaded".
Expect that the text of "#metric-users" is equal to "users: 42".
Expect that the text of "#metric-errors" is equal to "errors: 0".
Expect that "#error" is hidden.

## Test: a failed load shows the error, offers retry, and counts the failure

Given that GET "/api/metrics" answers 500 with "server exploded".
Click "#load".
Expect that the text of "#status" is equal to "failed".
Expect that the text of "#error" is equal to "Something went wrong. Try again.".
Expect that "#retry" is visible.
Expect that the text of "#fail-count" is equal to "1 failed fetches".
Expect that the count of "li" is equal to 0.

## Test: retrying after a failure can still succeed

Given that GET "/api/metrics" answers 500 with "server exploded".
Click "#load".
Expect that the text of "#status" is equal to "failed".
Given that GET "/api/metrics" answers 200 with "[{{\"label\": \"users\", \"value\": 7}}]".
Click "#retry".
Expect that the text of "#status" is equal to "loaded".
Expect that the text of "#metric-users" is equal to "users: 7".
Expect that "#error" is hidden.

## Test: the page-wide failure counter is independent of each call's own handling

Given that GET "/api/metrics" answers 500 with "first".
Click "#load".
Given that GET "/api/metrics" answers 500 with "second".
Click "#retry".
Expect that the text of "#fail-count" is equal to "2 failed fetches".
